  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 246 PM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nLow pressure currently resides over western Pennsylvania with broad \ncyclonic flow extending throughout the Great Lakes region. Broad \nlift combined with multiple subtle surface troughs are leading to \nscattered showers this afternoon. With cold air advection ongoing \naloft and steepening lapse rates, precipitation has been convective \nin nature. There have been a few reports of graupel at times. Mixed \nprecipitation will remain possible overnight as temperatures \ncontinue to cool.\n\nAdditionally, fetch off of Lake Michigan is maximized with \nnorthwesterly flow. This could allow for an enhanced band of \nprecipitation extending into the northern or northwestern portions \nof our CWA. Some snow may mix in especially within this lake band \ntonight into Friday morning.\n\nWinds remain elevated as the MSLP gradient around the low remains \ntight. Gusts between 20 to 30 mph are possible through tonight. \nThese winds should gradually diminish tomorrow as the low pulls \naway. Cloud cover may linger well into the afternoon and evening \nhours on Friday as cyclonic flow maintains enough weak lift to \nsupport stratocumulus.\n\nAs for temperatures a small diurnal spread is anticipated due to \nwind and cloud cover. Highs today in the 40s will drop into the 30s \novernight. Temperatures rebound into the upper 40s again on Friday. \nSkies clear over the weekend which will allow the temperature spread \nto widen. Good radiational cooling conditions will allow lows to dip \ninto the 20s both Friday night and Saturday night.\n\nOne caveat to the temperature forecast Saturday and Sunday is \nlingering northwesterly flow. A narrow band of cloud cover extending \ndownwind of Lake Michigan may persist so long as the fetch remains \noff the primary axis of the lake. These clouds will insulate the \nboundary layer keeping lows closer to 30 compared to the mid 20s \nelsewhere. Based on the expected wind field, this temperature \nuncertainty is greatest across our northeastern counties.\n\nMonday through the Middle of Next Week\n\nThe upper low will shift east into the Canadian Maritimes by Sunday \nwith an overall transition to broad ridging aloft that will persist \nfor much of next week. A strong high pressure ridge will pivot into \nthe Ohio Valley for the second half of the weekend with a return to \nquiet weather and mainly clear skies that will last through much of \nnext week. The onset of warm advection will usher in a slow warming \ntrend with seasonable conditions for mid November by the middle of \nnext week. Highs will largely remain in the mid and upper 40s \nthrough Monday...then rising into the mid and upper 50s by next \nWednesday and Thursday. Lows will range between the mid 20s and \nlower 30s through the period.",
